  "user_impact": "Screen reader users hear content in a different sequence than sighted users perceive visually.",
  "technical_impact": "CSS `order` changes the visual sequence while the DOM and accessibility tree stay unchanged.",
  "source": "WCAG 2.2 SC 1.3.2 Meaningful Sequence and SC 2.4.3 Focus Order; failure C27 (CSS positioning/order that changes meaning)",
  "applicability": "Rendered flex containers with flex-direction *-reverse, or whose children carry a non-zero CSS order, over content (text or tab stops).",
  "expectation": "needs_review once per container where CSS shows content in another order\nthan the DOM: row-reverse or column-reverse over two or more children with\ncontent, or a child with order != 0. Not reported: order: 0 (the default),\na single child, a teaser card whose tab stops all lead to the same\ndestination, containers not rendered. A reversal that applies only on the\nphone viewport, or is set by script after load, counts.",
  "method": "shared",
  "capabilities": [
    "dom",
    "computed_style"
  ],
  "page_level": false,
